Note = {Our Visual Landscape Conference on Visual Resource Management, ASCONA,
   SWITZERLAND, AUG, 1999},
Abstract = {In this paper, a conceptual framework is described for a better
   understanding of future landscapes as aesthetical objects. The paper is
   divided in four parts. In the first part, the poor aesthetic reality of
   today's landscapes is described and the consequences for aesthetic
   perception are explained. In the second part, a more sustainable use of
   landscape is discussed as developmental necessity for the next decades,
   and some aesthetic aspects of such a development are examined. In the
   third part, human aesthetic perception is described as a basic cognition
   process, differentiating between four major levels of knowledge or of
   sense (perception, expression, symptomatic information, and symbolic
   meaning). In the last part, all aspects of the first three parts are
   used to determine basic aesthetic categories of future landscapes. As
   the most relevant aesthetic categories are identified: the beautiful,
   the (new) sublime, the interesting, and the plain. Finally an attempt is
   made to derive from these categories the most important aesthetic
   prototypes of tomorrow's landscape. (C) 2001 Elsevier Science B.V. All
   rights reserved.},
