   COLUMBUS, OH, OCT 20-NOV 01, 1992},
Organization = {ASSOC COLLEGIATE SCH},
Abstract = {One of the problems faced by planners in the urban design process is
   that of communication, With a growing interest by the public in the
   participatory process, it is essential that graphic communication is
   simple, meaningful and vivid to provide adequate support to conventional
   verbal and written communication. The pioneering work of Kevin Lynch at
   Massachusetts Institute of Technology over three decades ago was among
   the first attempts to provide an understanding of the way in which the
   citizen understands the urban environment. Work by Lynch's colleagues,
   Gyorgy Kepes and Donald Appleyard, extends these attempts to encompass
   psychological data and an understanding of space/time and movement
   through the city. The latter was paralleled by Gordon Cullen in Britain
   at the same time with his concept of `serial vision'.
   The author, who studied and worked with all the above, describes his own
   extension of the techniques in those days. The paper suggests that these
   early attempts would have been greatly enhanced had present-day
   three-dimensional computer imaging and animation combined with video
   technology been available then and the struggle to formulate a
   satisfactory analytical and design system might have been easier. Three
   proposals for computer applications in urban design, including serial
   vision, are put forward and a case study of such applications to a
   medium sized American city completes the paper, which concludes with a
   perspective for future technology including computer-generated video
   animation.},
