Pages = {913-918},
Note = {International Conference on Sustainable Synergies from Buildings to the
   Urban Scale (SBE), Thessaloniki, GREECE, OCT 16-19, 2016},
Organization = {Aristotle Univ Thessaloniki, Fac Engn, Dept Civil Engn, Lab Building
   Construct \& Building Phys; Tech Chamber Greece},
Abstract = {The LIFE programme was established in 1992 and is the EU's funding
   instrument for the environment. The general objective of LIFE is to
   contribute to the implementation, updating and development of EU
   environmental policy and legislation by co-financing pilot or
   demonstration projects with added value.
   Since its start, LIFE has been continuously co-funding innovative
   projects to help address the EU's transition to a low-carbon and
   climate-resilient economy, strategically underpinning the implementation
   of the EU strategy on energy and climate change related targets.
   LIFE has strongly supported renewable energy and energy-efficiency
   projects, as well as innovative techniques that increase urban
   resilience within the construction sector, thus significantly
   contributing to the implementation of EU climate change, renewable
   energy and energy efficiency policy.
   Some LIFE projects have demonstrated the benefits of the use or re-use
   in construction of local organic materials, such as straw, wood, or reed
   to renovate and improve the thermal efficiency of buildings. Another
   cluster of LIFE projects showed the use of automated controls to
   optimise the management of heating, cooling and electricity in
   buildings. Other awareness-raising projects focused on teaching
   construction professionals the most up-to-date techniques for renovation
   and retrofitting as well as informing them about materials and
   technologies that promote energy efficiency. Finally, LIFE has developed
   and deployed innovative adaptation techniques within the building sector
   to increase urban resilience to climate change impacts, such as water
   scarcity, flooding and heat island effects. (C) 2017 The Authors.
   Published by Elsevier B.V.},
